## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical data structure design for a common gaming feature, balancing efficient updates with query performance.

## Key Concepts
- Hash maps provide O(1) player score lookups
- Ordered maps enable range and top-k access
- Ordered sets handle duplicate scores within groups
- Rank computation accounts for tied scores

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating approach
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(){
    this.playerToScore = new Map();
    this.scoreToPlayers = new Map(); // descending keys; each maps to a Set of players
  }
  addOrUpdate(playerId, score) {
    // Remove from old score group if exists
    const oldScore = this.playerToScore.get(playerId);
    if (oldScore !== undefined) {
      const oldSet = this.scoreToPlayers.get(oldScore);
      oldSet.delete(playerId);
      if (oldSet.size === 0) this.scoreToPlayers.delete(oldScore);
    }
    // Update maps and add to new score group
    this.playerToScore.set(playerId, score);
    if (!this.scoreToPlayers.has(score)) {
      this.scoreToPlayers.set(score, new Set());
    }
    this.scoreToPlayers.get(score).add(playerId);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of advanced data structure design by combining segment trees with multisets to handle weighted intervals with duplicate support. It also evaluates understanding of binary search applied to aggregated data structures for kth largest queries.

## Key Concepts
- **Coordinate compression** on endpoints to discretize the domain
- **Segment tree** where each node stores a multiset of weights for intervals covering that node's range
- **Interval decomposition** into O(log n) nodes for efficient updates
- **Path aggregation** for point sum queries
- **Binary search over weight values** with counts from multisets to support kthLargest queries

## Code Example
```javascript
class IntervalDS {
  constructor(coords) {
    this.coords = coords;
    this.N = coords.length;
    this.tree = Array(4 * this.N).fill(null).map(() => new Map());
    this.sums = Array(4 * this.N).fill(0);
  }

  insert(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, 1);
  }

  erase(l, r, w) {
    this.update(1, 0, this.N - 1, l, r, w, -1);
  }

  update(node, start, end, l, r, w, delta) {
    if (r < start || end < l) return;
    if (l <= start && end <= r) {
      const count = this.tree[node].get(w) || 0;
      this.tree[node].set(w, count + delta);
      if (this.tree[node].get(w) === 0) this.tree[node].delete(w);
      this.sums[node] += w * delta;
      return;
    }
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.update(node * 2, start, mid, l, r, w, delta);
    this.update(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, l, r, w, delta);
    this.sums[node] = this.sums[node * 2] + this.sums[node * 2 + 1];
  }

  sumAtPoint(x) {
    return this.querySum(1, 0, this.N - 1, x);
  }

  querySum(node, start, end, x) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return 0;
    if (start === end) return this.sums[node];
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    return this.sums[node] + 
      (x <= mid ? this.querySum(node * 2, start, mid, x) 
                : this.querySum(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x));
  }

  kthLargest(x, k) {
    const weights = [...new Set(this.getWeightsAtPoint(x))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
    if (k > weights.length) return null;
    return weights[k - 1];
  }

  getWeightsAtPoint(x) {
    const result = [];
    this.collectWeights(1, 0, this.N - 1, x, result);
    return result;
  }

  collectWeights(node, start, end, x, result) {
    if (x < start || x > end) return;
    for (const [w, count] of this.tree[node]) {
      result.push(...Array(count).fill(w));
    }
    if (start === end) return;
    const mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2, start, mid, x, result);
    this.collectWeights(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, x, result);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple data structure with guaranteed O(1) operations and handle edge cases like duplicates and emptiness.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack technique to keep current minimum per depth
- Constant-time top and getMin
- Correct empty-state handling and error signaling

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.mins = [];
  }
  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    if (this.mins.length === 0) {
      this.mins.push(x);
    } else {
      this.mins.push(Math.min(x, this.mins[this.mins.length - 1]));
    }
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    this.stack.pop();
    this.mins.pop();
  }
  top() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }
  getMin() {
    if (this.mins.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.mins[this.mins.length - 1];
  }
}
```

